#
# fetchconfig.pl - CHANGES
#

9.59
============
+ Abstract.pm: shared SSH transport - ssh_open(), open_ssh_debug_file()
  and the ssh_extra_opts() hook - replacing the 25-44-line connection
  block that each of the seven SSH models carried (ArubaCXSSH,
  NexusSSH, PLANET, ProCurveSSH, CiscoIOSSSH, CiscoASASSH, ComwareSSH;
  502 lines -> 42, Abstract.pm +138). The block does the same job
  everywhere: Net::OpenSSH connection (password, no host-key
  verification - see SECURITY NOTES), the setsid warning filter (now
  a scope guard that restores the previous __WARN__ handler),
  open2pty, and the Net::Telnet wrapper (Errmode return, Timeout,
  Telnetmode 0, Cmd_remove_mode 1, output record separator CR).
  Net::OpenSSH is loaded on first use, so a telnet-only installation
  still need not have it.
  What stays per model is device knowledge: the legacy crypto an
  older switch needs, declared with ssh_extra_opts(): ProCurveSSH and
  ComwareSSH re-enable diffie-hellman-group14-sha1 and group1-sha1,
  ssh-rsa host keys and aes128-cbc/3des-cbc; CiscoASASSH the same but
  with group1-sha1 only. Preserved byte-for-byte.
  The copies had diverged in one way that was a gap, now closed: the
  three newest models wrote a complete .debug file (the ssh client's
  -v trace, then the session dump, mode 0600), ProCurveSSH wrote only
  the session dump, and CiscoIOSSSH, CiscoASASSH and ComwareSSH
  honoured debug=on for nothing at the SSH layer - no .debug file at
  all, although the README documented one. All seven now produce the
  full file. Cmd_remove_mode is set for ProCurveSSH too; it only
  affects Net::Telnet cmd(), which no SSH model calls.
  Verified offline by capturing the exact argument lists passed to
  Net::OpenSSH->new and Net::Telnet->new, old vs new, for every model
  with debug off and on: identical for Aruba/Nexus/PLANET in both
  modes and for all seven with debug off (CiscoIOSSSH differs only in
  the order of its -o options, which ssh ignores); with debug on the
  four older models differ exactly by gaining LogLevel=VERBOSE +
  master_stderr_fh and dump_log - the declared change. The harness
  also caught a real defect in the first version of ssh_open: the
  indirect-object form "new Net::Telnet(...)" copied from the models
  is parsed as a function call in Abstract.pm (which does not "use
  Net::Telnet"); fixed to Net::Telnet->new before release. Needs live
  verification on all seven SSH models (see below).
  README: debug documented for cisco-ios-ssh/cisco-asa-ssh and
  comware-ssh (new), procurve-ssh entry made precise.
+ expect_enable_prompt: final migration. Parks and Riverstone (tail
  "# $") passed a step label as the third argument, which the shared
  function reads as the tail override; the shared function gained an
  optional fourth $label parameter (used only in the error message,
  e.g. "fetching-config: could not match ...") and the three call
  sites pass (undef, label). Acme's single call already passes its
  per-command delimiter "[#>]" as the tail override, so only its copy
  was deleted. Mediant's prompt is "/CONFiguration>"; its caller
  escaped the leading "/" by hand ("\\" . $prompt) because the copy
  interpolated the prompt raw - the shared quoting covers "/", so the
  manual escape is gone and prompt_tail is "$". MikroTik's copy was
  dead code (never called; the end of the configuration is found by
  expect_enable_prompt_paging) and was removed. Verified per model
  with OLD vs NEW through a real Net::Telnet on device-shaped byte
  streams: byte-identical prematch/match for Parks, Riverstone (both
  call sites), Acme (both "#" and ">" prompt signs) and Mediant, and
  identical rejection of a prompt without the trailing space.
  Deliberately NOT migrated: CiscoSG300, whose copy ignores the learned
  prompt and matches "\S+#$" - moving it onto the shared function would
  tighten the match to the learned hostname, a behaviour change that
  cannot be verified without an SG300 (the loose match may exist
  because the prompt of a stacked SG300 varies with its role). It is
  now the only model with a private expect_enable_prompt, documented
  as such in the file. 26 copies -> 1 shared + 1 deliberate exception.
+ Decision, hirschmann: the "users passwd <user> :v1:<hash>:" lines of
  "show running-config all" are re-rendered by the Railswitch on every
  read (fixed salt prefix, different body), so every backup compares as
  changed and changes_only never discards one for this model. A
  config_equal override ignoring those lines was considered and
  REJECTED by the maintainer: ignoring them would also hide a real
  password change. Accepted cost: one saved backup per run for
  Hirschmann devices (bounded by keep=N). Recorded here so it is not
  "fixed" later by mistake.
+ FIX: the ssh trace in the .debug file was effectively empty. The
  SSH master ran at LogLevel=VERBOSE, which the header line and the
  README described as the "-v" authentication trace - but VERBOSE is
  not -v: OpenSSH's levels are QUIET FATAL ERROR INFO VERBOSE DEBUG
  DEBUG1..3, "-v" is DEBUG1 (= DEBUG), and VERBOSE adds only a
  handful of messages above INFO. On AIX OpenSSH 8.1 it added
  nothing: a live .debug file held just the known-hosts warning and
  the switch's login banner (grep -c debug1: -> 0), while "ssh -v" on
  the same host produced the full trace. ssh_open now uses
  LogLevel=DEBUG when debug=on, so the file records key exchange, host
  key, authentication methods tried and "Authentication succeeded" -
  the information needed when a legacy switch refuses a kex. DEBUG
  rather than DEBUG2/3 to avoid per-packet noise. ssh never logs the
  password. Header line and SECURITY NOTES corrected; this has been
  the behaviour since the debug file was introduced (aruba/planet),
  not a 9.59 regression.

9.58
============
+ Abstract.pm: shared expect_enable_prompt() with prompt_head() /
  prompt_tail() hooks. 26 models each carried a private copy of the
  same 12-line function; a normalised diff showed 19 distinct bodies
  whose only difference is the regex tail after the hostname ("#$",
  "# ?$", " > $", "> \(enable\) $", a parenthesised or bracketed
  hostname, ...) - one line of device knowledge inside a dozen lines
  of scaffolding. The scaffolding now lives in Abstract; a model
  contributes its device fact by overriding prompt_tail() (and
  prompt_head() for prompts that wrap the hostname). Defaults
  reproduce the classic "hostname#" case. The prompt is quoted
  byte-safely (regexp_quote_keep_bytes): 21 of the old copies
  interpolated it raw, so a "." in a hostname matched any character
  and "(", "[" or "+" would have broken the match. An optional
  per-call $tail_override serves models whose prompt sign varies per
  command. A model that still defines its own copy keeps using it, so
  migration is one model at a time.
+ First migration: CiscoIOS, CiscoIOSSSH, Coriant8600 and DmSwitch
  (all default tail "#$") lost their private copies and inherit the
  shared one; no other change to those models. Verified two ways: an
  offline equivalence harness comparing the old and new regex over a
  corpus of prompt lines, and a live comparison driving the OLD copy
  (from the PRE-prompt restore point) and the NEW implementation
  through a real Net::Telnet on identical byte streams - byte-identical
  prematch/match for all four models, including a banner line ending
  in "#" (stays in the prematch) and a trailing-space prompt (correctly
  rejected by "#$", same error text). The remaining 22 models are
  unchanged and will be migrated individually with the same harness.
+ Second migration (13 models): Dell, Zyxel and PLANET (tail "# ?$"),
  CiscoASA, CiscoASASSH, CiscoPIX and TellabsMSR ("# $"), CiscoCAT
  ("> \(enable\) $"), NECUnivergeIX ("[#\$] $"), Terayon (unanchored
  "#"), CiscoIOSXR (default "#$"), Hirschmann (head "\(", tail
  "\)\s?#$") and 3ComMSR (head "\[", tail "[^\[\]]*\]$"). Each lost
  its private copy and declares its device fact as a one-line
  prompt_tail() (plus prompt_head() where the prompt wraps the
  hostname). CiscoASA/CiscoASASSH/CiscoIOSXR used to escape "/" in the
  prompt by hand (IOS-XR prompts such as "RP/0/RSP0/CPU0:asr9k#");
  the shared quoting covers "/" so that step is gone. TellabsMSR's
  copy had two extra debug lines ("waiting"/"found enable command
  prompt") which the shared version does not emit. Verified per
  model: the constructed regex string is identical to the old one, and
  OLD vs NEW driven through a real Net::Telnet on identical byte
  streams give byte-identical prematch/match for the parenthesised,
  bracketed, CatOS, slash, "$"-sign, unanchored and spaced-hostname
  cases. Confirmed live by the maintainer on cisco-ios and
  cisco-ios-ssh after the first migration.
  Still carrying a private copy (deliberately, each needs its own
  handling): Parks and Riverstone (pass an error LABEL as the third
  argument, which the shared function would read as a tail
  override), Acme (per-call delimiter), Mediant (caller pre-escapes
  the prompt with "\\"), MikroTik (the learned prompt is the whole
  match including " > "), CiscoSG300 (ignores the learned prompt and
  matches "\S+#$"), ProCurve and ProCurveSSH (VT100 escape prefix).
+ Abstract.pm: single stripansi(), replacing three per-model copies
  (ProCurve.pm, ProCurveSSH.pm, ComwareSSH.pm; 12 call sites, now
  fetchconfig::model::Abstract::stripansi). The copies had diverged:
  the ProCurve two carried the DEC private-mode rule (ESC[?25h/l) and
  the ESC E rule, ComwareSSH's was frozen at the older two-rule form.
  The shared version is a strict superset with two corrections: the
  CSI rule accepts any number of ";"-separated parameters (the old
  "\d*;?\d*" allowed at most two, so an SGR such as ESC[1;37;40m
  survived it), and NUL bytes are stripped alongside CR. Verified by
  recording the old copies' output on a 161 KB corpus - the ProCurve
  2610 telnet dump (cursor addressing, scroll regions, ?25h/?25l,
  ESC E), ProCurve SSH prompt captures, the complete 80 KB Comware
  SSH session (zero escapes, all 3,387 lines "\r\r\n", two NULs at
  the banner boundaries), plus synthetic edge cases - and comparing
  the new function against each: every difference is the new
  function REMOVING bytes the old one left (NULs; 3+-parameter CSI;
  for the Comware copy, the ?25l/?25h/ESC E it never had). No case
  adds or changes a byte, and on every real capture the ProCurve
  output is identical. Saved configurations of all three models are
  unaffected.
+ Third migration: ProCurve and ProCurveSSH lost their private
  expect_enable_prompt (identical copies) and declare
  prompt_head => "(?:\x1b\[[\d;?]*[A-Za-z])*" (the cursor escapes the
  switch emits right before the prompt, consumed as part of the prompt
  unit) and prompt_tail => "# " (unanchored, as before). The
  constructed regex is identical to the old one for the real prompts
  ("swi11024_swi320059", "IS Nr 337583"); for a non-ASCII hostname it
  now differs on purpose: the old copy used quotemeta, which yields the
  broken "\<c3>\<bc>" form fixed on aruba-cx-ssh, while the shared
  version keeps the bytes literal. Verified OLD vs NEW on a real
  Net::Telnet with the 2610 post-command escape sequence, the SSH
  ESC[1H prompt and the ESC[?25l prompt: byte-identical prematch and
  match. Remaining private copies: Acme, CiscoSG300, Mediant,
  MikroTik, Parks, Riverstone (each needs its own handling).

+ Parallel fetching: new option "-P N" fetches with N concurrent
  worker processes (default 1 = sequential, unchanged behaviour). The
  fetch is now a separate phase: Detector::parse only registers
  devices (with their source file/line), and Detector::fetch_device()
  does the per-device work that used to run inline while the table
  was read; fetchconfig.pl's run_fetch_phase() drives it. At -P 1 the
  loop runs in device-table order (device_ids_in_order) and the output
  is identical to earlier versions (verified: same banner order, same
  per-device line count as the 9.57 build). With N>1 the parent hands
  out one device at a time over a pipe to whichever worker asks next;
  each worker is a fork of the fully loaded parent, so no model needed
  changes.
  Output is atomic per device: a worker redirects its STDOUT/STDERR
  into per-device temporary buffers for the duration of a fetch, and
  the parent alone copies each finished device's buffers to the real
  streams as one uninterrupted block, so one device's lines are never
  interleaved with another's (verified mechanically over an 8-device
  -P 4 run with staggered durations: zero violations). Blocks appear in
  completion order.
  Results: Mailer::record_result cannot share @results across
  processes, so while a result sink is set (set_result_sink) a worker
  serializes each row to a per-device file (undef, tabs, newlines and
  backslashes round-trip exactly); the parent replays it
  (replay_result_file) through record_result, so the summary mail,
  per-device to= bundling and the .status files are produced by the
  parent exactly as at -P 1.
  Robustness: a worker that dies is detected via waitpid; the device
  it held is recorded as failed (mail + .status) and the remaining
  devices are fetched by the surviving workers (verified by SIGKILLing
  a worker mid-fetch: 7 fetched, 1 failed, run completed). -P is
  refused together with the lookup/maintenance modes and must be a
  positive integer. The repository is safe under concurrency: each
  device owns its directory, .status and .debug; only the shared date
  directories are created by several workers, which make_path
  tolerates.
  README: new PARALLEL FETCHING section (how it works, atomic
  output/completion order, sizing N); usage text and HTML updated.
+ FIX, ProCurve.pm / ProCurveSSH.pm: the learned prompt carried a
  fragment of a cursor escape. The switch emits ESC[1H (cursor home)
  right before the hostname; the prompt token [\w.-]+ was allowed to
  start INSIDE that escape, so ESC and "[" were left in the prematch
  (and saved as two junk lines - a bare "\x1b[" - at the END of the
  backup) while "1H" was swallowed into the hostname: the prompt was
  learned as "1HIS Nr 337583" and logged that way. It still worked
  only because the same escape preceded every later prompt, which
  also meant any change in the switch's terminal handling would have
  broken the end-of-config match. wait_for_command_prompt() now
  matches any escape sequence(s) as part of the prompt unit
  ((?:\x1b\[[\d;?]*[A-Za-z])* before the pattern), the post-checks
  and the learned name are taken from stripansi() of that match, and
  the later prompt regexps accept the same optional escape prefix.
  Result: the prompt is learned as the true hostname ("IS Nr
  337583"), the saved backup no longer ends in escape junk (11 lines
  instead of 13 in the test), and the behaviour is IDENTICAL whether
  or not the switch emits the escape (verified with the mock both
  ways; unchanged-comparison and -P 3 atomic output intact). Log
  lines "found prompt" / "found end of configuration" show the
  stripped text.
+ FIX, ProCurve.pm (telnet) - regression from the escape handling
  above, seen live on swi11024: the switch clears the screen with a
  run of 23 CRLFs and then emits ESC[?25l (DEC "hide cursor") before
  the prompt. The prompt regex now consumed that escape as part of
  the match, but stripansi()'s private-mode rule stripped only the
  "h" (set) form, not "l" (reset), so "25l" survived and was
  swallowed into the hostname ("25lswi11024_swi320059"); the "no
  page" prompt match then failed and the fetch aborted. The CRLF run
  was also printed raw inside the "found command prompt" debug line -
  the "huge empty space" in the screen output. stripansi() now strips
  ESC[?...h AND ESC[?...l (ProCurve.pm and ProCurveSSH.pm), and every
  "found command prompt" debug line shows the stripped text. Verified
  against a telnet mock replaying the exact swi11024 sequence: clean
  hostname, "no page" accepted, 7-line config saved with zero escape
  or CR bytes, no blank lines in the log.
+ FIX, ProCurve.pm: a switch that answers "show run" with nothing but
  its prompt crashed the run ("Modification of non-creatable array
  value attempted, subscript -1" at the trailing-escape strip of the
  last config line) instead of the fetch failing cleanly as an empty
  configuration. Guarded. Found while building the mock above.
+ FIX (first live -P 4 run): "could not open dir: <repo>/<dev>.status.tmp.<pid>:
  No such file or directory" followed by "latest config not found",
  after which changes_only saved an UNCHANGED config. A race that
  only exists under -P: the parent writes device A's .status file
  (temp file + atomic rename in the repository root) while a worker's
  scan_dir lists that root for device B. The worker's readdir saw the
  temp name, the rename then removed it, -f failed, and the old code
  treated a non-file as a directory to recurse into; the failed
  opendir was escalated to a fatal scan error. Two fixes: (1)
  scan_dir skips any entry that is neither a regular file nor a
  directory at stat time (vanished, socket, broken symlink) instead of
  recursing into it, and treats an opendir failing with ENOENT as "no
  backups here" rather than an error - under -P, entries appearing
  and disappearing in shared directories is normal, and a repository
  that does not exist yet on a device's first run is the same case;
  (2) the .status temp file is now a dotfile
  (".<dev_id>.status.tmp.<pid>"), which scan_dir ignores by name, so
  the race cannot even arise. Verified with a deterministic
  reproduction (an entry that is gone by stat time): the pre-fix
  scan_dir fails with exactly the live error, the fixed one returns
  the correct file set with no error; a -P 4 rerun shows 8/8
  unchanged configs discarded and zero errors, atomic output intact.

9.57
============
+ New model NexusSSH.pm (label nexus-ssh) for Cisco Nexus switches
  (NX-OS; observed on 10.4(7)) over SSH. Modeled on ArubaCXSSH.pm
  (Net::OpenSSH pty driven through Net::Telnet, shared prompt
  handling). From a real PuTTY session log: SSH authentication lands
  on "<hostname># " after the MOTD banner and the NX-OS copyright text;
  NX-OS has no Cisco-IOS-style "enable", so a ">" prompt is reported
  as an error naming the reason. Pager disabled with "terminal length
  0"; "show running-config" opens with "!Command:", "!Running
  configuration last done at:" and "!Time:" comment lines, which are
  kept in the backup; "!Time:" changes on every run and is ignored in
  the comparison (config_equal_ignoring_lines), while "!Running
  configuration last done at:" changes only with the configuration
  and deliberately counts. The start of the config is verified against
  "!Command:"; NX-OS's "^" error pointer is recognized alongside "%".
  "exit" closes the session. debug=on writes the 0600 debug file with
  the ssh authentication record and the session dump. Registered in
  Detector.pm; default block and sample device in device_table.example;
  README (intro, model table, options) and HTML documentation updated.
  Verified against a real sshd whose login shell replays the captured
  session: backup starts at "!Command:", a second run with a different
  "!Time:" is discarded as unchanged, a real change is kept, ">"
  prompt and wrong password fail cleanly, debug file is 0600.
+ PERFORMANCE, Abstract::scan_dir: finding one device's backups used to
  walk the ENTIRE repository tree, descending into every other
  device's directory and stat()ing every file, discarding all but one
  device's. find_latest calls it once per device per run (for
  changes_only and keep=N expiry), and -Z/-S/-o call it once per
  device too. Measured on a synthetic repository of 2,500 devices x 30
  backups (150k inodes, warm cache): 704 ms per device, i.e. ~29
  minutes of pure directory walking per nightly run and again per
  maintenance check. The layout is <repo>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<dev_id>/,
  so at the day level scan_dir now opens only "<day>/<dev_id>" and
  skips the day if that directory is absent (ENOENT, the cheap common
  case). Month directories and anything not matching the known layout
  are still walked as before, so oddly placed files remain
  discoverable. Result: 0.41 ms per device (~1,700x); a 2,500-device
  run spends ~1 s here instead of ~29 min; -Z for 20 devices went
  from 13.7 s to 0.18 s end-to-end. Results are identical to the old
  walk (verified side-by-side for 100 devices and for files at
  non-standard depths); the real fetch/unchanged/expiry paths and
  -l/-g/-Z/-s/-z regression-tested unchanged. No layout change, no
  migration, no new dependency.
+ README introduction: the device list stopped at NEC Univerge IX and
  omitted every model added since; it now names them all.

9.56
============
This entry consolidates every change from the 9.x fork (9.25 through
9.56) into one description of how 9.56 differs from upstream 0.25.
Intermediate states that were later superseded are not listed.

MODELS AND TRANSPORTS
+ SSH counterparts of the telnet models, driving the shell through a
  Net::OpenSSH pseudo-terminal wrapped in Net::Telnet so the existing
  prompt handling is reused: cisco-ios-ssh, cisco-asa-ssh, comware-ssh,
  procurve-ssh. SSH host keys are deliberately not verified
  (StrictHostKeyChecking=no, UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null): the job
  runs unattended and a switch's host key changes on every hardware
  swap; documented in SECURITY NOTES.
+ New telnet/SSH models: comware-ssh (HPE Comware/H3C), hirschmann
  (Hirschmann Railswitch; "(<name>) >"/"#" prompt, no enable password,
  config anchored on "!Current Configuration:"), zyxel (Zyxel switches,
  Cisco-IOS-like CLI, full "terminal length 0"/"show running-config"
  spelling), planet-ssh (PLANET managed switches; both firmware
  variants - with and without the switch's own Username:/Password:
  dialog after SSH login, with or without a "Building configuration..."
  banner, one or two "exit"s to leave; input is typed with a
  configurable inter-character pause, type_delay, default 100 ms,
  because the switch drops bursts arriving right after a prompt),
  aruba-cx-ssh (Aruba CX / AOS-CX; administrator account required, no
  "enable"; also backs up HPE Aruba AOS-8 access points).
+ New web-UI transport (AbstractHTTP.pm, LWP::UserAgent with cookie
  jar) for switches without a CLI: tplink-web-sg105e (TP-Link Easy
  Smart TL-SG105E/PE, both the IP-bound and the cookie-bound session
  variants) and procurve-web-1700 (HP ProCurve 1700). The binary
  config is stored uuencoded so it fits the text repository,
  changes_only comparison and -g retrieval; restore with uudecode.
  debug=on records every request/response with the password masked.
+ New SNMP+TFTP transport, procurve-snmp (HP ProCurve ProVision incl.
  the 4000M/8000M chassis): an SNMP set arms the switch's TFTP export,
  the config is fetched by TFTP, a second set disarms it - always,
  even after a failed transfer. Credential is the SNMP read-write
  community (community=, no user/pass); remote_file= defaults to
  "browse" (readable display export), later firmware also accepts
  "running-config"; oid= and snmp_version= are overridable. Requires
  Net::SNMP and Net::TFTP.
+ Prompt handling made robust across real devices: hostnames with
  spaces (ProCurve "IS Nr 337542#", Aruba "apgmg01 UG IT-Buero#") and
  non-ASCII characters are matched by a whole-line, newline-bounded
  token; the learned prompt is quoted byte-safely
  (regexp_quote_keep_bytes, not quotemeta, which mangles multi-byte
  UTF-8) and normalized to raw bytes; aruba-cx-ssh waits for output to
  go idle (prompt_settle_ms, default 150) before matching so a banner
  line ending in "#" is not mistaken for the prompt. ProCurve login
  handles the "Press any key to continue" banner, the "save current
  configuration [y/n]" prompt, and confirms "no page" before fetching.
+ Volatile lines that must not count as a change are ignored in the
  comparison per model (Abstract::config_equal_ignoring_lines): the
  Cisco ASA "!!: Written by ..." save timestamp, the PLANET "! System
  Up Time:" header. Comware/ProCurve/Aruba banner and header lines
  ("Building configuration...", "Current configuration:", "Running
  configuration:") are stripped by anchor, not by a blind line count.
+ A fetch that fails partway is never saved as an empty backup:
  dump_config refuses undefined/empty content, Detector treats a
  0-byte saved file as a failed fetch and discards it, and the three
  models that used to call dump_config unconditionally no longer do.

BACKUP FILES, NAMING AND ORDERING
+ Saved configuration files end with a trailing newline on the last
  line, matching the device's own output (before, the last line had
  none). The first run after upgrading reports every device as
  "changed" once.
+ filename_append_suffix must start with "." (a file extension); a
  dotless suffix, or one containing "/" or whitespace, fails that
  device's backup with an error and writes nothing. Do not add or
  change a suffix once a device has backups (old and new names would
  coexist; see -s/-S).
+ Every operation that orders backups ("latest" for changes_only,
  keep=N expiry, -g/-n/-m, -l, -z/-Z, -s/-S) sorts by the parsed
  timestamp YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS, not by a string sort of the whole name,
  so neither the timezone token nor a suffix can disturb the order.
  One shared parser (Abstract::parse_backup_filename) recognizes every
  timezone form strftime produces - "+0200" (Linux, modern Windows), a
  bare name such as "CEST"/"CET"/"NFT"/"DFT" that changes with DST
  (AIX), "-CEST" (Solaris), a multi-word name such as "W. Europe
  Standard Time" (older Windows), or none (timezone=hide).
+ Backup filenames have one-second resolution; two backups of one
  device within the same second would share a name. Accepted and
  documented (BACKUP FILE NAMING): a real fetch never completes in
  under a second.
+ timeout defaults to 30 seconds when set neither on the device line
  nor the model's default: line, and says so at debug level ("Using
  30s fetch timeout because the timeout was not set for this model").
  Before, an unset timeout fell through to the library's own default
  (10 s telnet/SSH, 180 s web UI).

COMMAND-LINE MAINTENANCE TOOLS
+ -g dev_id [-n N] retrieves a backed-up config (1 = latest); -m M
  with -g prints a diff between backup N and older backup M; -l dev_id
  lists a device's backups; -z dev_id / -Z check one / every loaded
  device for zero-byte backups; -f file writes the result data to a
  file. Exit status carries the result (1 = something found / failed).
+ -o finds orphaned backups - device directories on disk with no
  matching dev_id in the loaded device table - across every
  configured repository= path. -o -D deletes them: each file by its
  exact name (unlink, logged "rm <file>"), then the directory by one
  plain rmdir, then the day (YYYYMMDD) and month (YYYYMM) directories
  this leaves empty; never a wildcard, never a recursive delete. -T
  previews the commands without deleting.
+ -e finds empty directories in the <repo>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<dev_id>/
  layout, bottom-up, listing each as level (device/day/month) and
  path; -e -D removes them bottom-up by one plain rmdir each, every
  command reported; -e -D -T previews. Only names of the expected
  shape are ever candidates; the repository root is never touched.
  -D therefore requires -o or -e.
+ -s dev_id checks that all of a device's backups carry the same
  filename suffix and, if so, that it matches the configured
  filename_append_suffix (exit 0 consistent+matching, 1 inconsistent,
  2 consistent but not matching); -S checks every loaded device for
  consistency only (exit 0/1). The suffix is recognized after the
  timezone token when separated by ".", "-" or "_".
+ The listing/check modes (-l, -z, -Z, -o, -e, -s, -S) end their
  result data with one summary line prefixed "#" (the same text the
  run logs), so a check that finds nothing still writes a non-empty,
  self-describing -f file. Parsers of these listings must skip lines
  starting with "#". -f receives only the result data; all
  info/debug/error lines go to stderr.
+ -?, -h and --help print the usage to stdout and exit 0.
+ device_table options may be quoted in double quotes to contain a
  comma (Text::ParseWords); new free-text comment= option; -devices=
  and -line= may be repeated.

E-MAIL SUMMARY (fetchconfig/Mailer.pm)
+ End-of-run summary e-mail as an HTML table (device, date, time,
  duration, success/failed, size, changed/unchanged/forced/n-a) with a
  statistics table above it, configured by an "email:" line
  (from=, to=, smtp=, optional user=/password= for AUTH LOGIN). No mail
  and no SMTP connection unless an "email:" line is present; none for
  the lookup modes. Per-device to= overrides the recipient; those
  devices are bundled into their own e-mail while the global recipient
  always gets everything.
+ email: options port= (TLS-aware default: 25 for off/starttls, 465
  for ssl), tls=off|starttls|ssl (needs IO::Socket::SSL only when
  enabled; if TLS is requested but unavailable the mail is refused,
  never downgraded to plaintext), footer= (the footer's copyright
  text; " - Version <n>" is fixed). Email-line values may be quoted
  to contain a comma. From/To/Subject have line breaks stripped
  (header-injection guard).
+ Rendering: colorized tables with inline styles, "failed" in red and
  "changed" in blue, Outlook-safe line heights and spacer, generic
  sans-serif font, Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit.

STATUS FILE
+ After every backup attempt <repository>/<dev_id>.status is written
  (INI-style: [<dev_id>], backup_start_date/time, backup_end_date/
  time, backup_duration_sec, backup_state=success|failed|skipped,
  backup_changed=unchanged|changed|forced|unknown), atomically,
  unconditionally (no email: line needed), for every model. Meant for
  external tools such as fetchconfig-web.

SECURITY
+ dev_id is validated (letters, digits, "_", ".", "-"; not dots-only,
  which would resolve to the repository or its parent) before it is
  used in paths, regexes or logs; pass=/enable=/community= are masked
  in every logged device-table line; shell-based mkdir replaced by
  File::Path::make_path; two-argument open() replaced by the
  three-argument form.
+ Session debug logging is opt-in per device (debug=on), written to
  <repository>/<dev_id>.debug with mode 0600 regardless of umask,
  because it records credentials verbatim; for the SSH models the
  file opens with the ssh client's own account of the authentication.
+ New README/HTML SECURITY NOTES section (debug-file credentials, the
  deliberate no-host-key-check policy, dev_id rules, SMTP/AUTH LOGIN).

CORE FIXES
+ Abstract::dump_config no longer leaks $, (output field separator)
  into later prints; the second-level dump_log filename typo
  ("<dev_id>..debug") fixed; ProCurve "-- MORE --" pagination handled
  in the SSH model; Zyxel/ProCurve prompts tolerate an optional
  trailing space.

DOCUMENTATION
+ README rewritten and extended (PERL MODULE REQUIREMENTS with the
  core-vs-non-core split per transport, every one of the 32 models
  with an options block headed by its label, EMAIL NOTIFICATIONS,
  RETRIEVING A PREVIOUSLY BACKED UP CONFIG incl. all maintenance
  tools, WEB UI BACKUPS, PROCURVE SNMP BACKUPS, SECURITY NOTES, BACKUP
  FILE NAMING, STATUS FILE), American English. README.retrieve merged
  in. New fetchconfig-documentation.html, shipped in the tarball,
  mirroring the README with a per-model option matrix that agrees with
  each model's actual options.
+ Copyright "Rainer Tammer"; Mailer footer default "(c) 2006-2026
  Everton da Silva Marques, Rainer Tammer and others".

